HST Office on Windows (and macOS): numbers follow the user's regional settings, like the leading
spreadsheet application (owner decision 2026-11-13).

- i18npool localedata.cxx: while the locale setting in Tools - Options - Languages and Locales is
  "Default" (ooSetupSystemLocale empty), the locale data of the OS user locale carries the decimal,
  thousands and list separator of the OS: Windows GetLocaleInfoEx(LOCALE_NAME_USER_DEFAULT,
  LOCALE_SDECIMAL/LOCALE_STHOUSAND/LOCALE_SLIST), so values customized in Control Panel - Region -
  Additional settings count too; macOS the CFLocale decimal and grouping separator (list ';' when
  the decimal is ',', else ','). The built-in number format codes (fixed, percent, scientific,
  currency) of that locale are rewritten to those separators, so typed numbers, displayed numbers
  and number formats agree. All of LibreOffice reads separators through this service
  (LocaleDataWrapper, the number formatter, the character classification parser), so this is the
  one place to change. An explicitly chosen locale, any other locale and Linux keep the LibreOffice
  locale data. en-US keeps its decimal and thousands separator: it is the invariant locale the
  .xlsx/.xls filters convert number format codes from and to; its list separator follows the OS.
  Unusable OS values (not one character, decimal equal to thousands) leave the data unchanged.
  Read once per process: a change in the regional settings applies on the next start.
- sc formulaopt.cxx: under the same condition the default formula separators are the leading
  application's: the argument separator is the OS list separator, ';' when it equals the decimal
  separator; array columns '\' when the decimal is ',', else ','; array rows ';' (Office 2021
  International(xlListSeparator/xlColumnSeparator/xlRowSeparator), measured 2026-11-13 on Windows 11
  for vi-VN, de-DE, fr-FR, es-ES and en-US, default and customized). Separators the user stored in
  Tools - Options - Sheets - Formula still win.
- i18npool Library_i18npool.mk: macOS links CoreFoundation.

ODF and .xlsx stay locale-independent: cell values, OpenFormula/OOXML formulas and .xlsx number
format codes are written with '.' and ',' whatever the separators, ODF number styles are structural.

License: these upstream files remain under their original license (MPL-2.0).
Test: tests/unit/test_windows_regional_separators.py,
tests/runtime/check-windows-regional-separators.py (Windows, installed build).
